[
    {
        "content": "<p>Mathlib contains both <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Graph#doc\">docs#Graph</a> and <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=SimpleGraph#doc\">docs#SimpleGraph</a>, but <code>SimpleGraph</code> is not an instance of <code>Graph</code>. For the sake of generality and possibly re-using some API, I would expect <code>SimpleGraph</code> to extend <code>Graph</code>. Specifically, if we defined <code>SimpleGraph</code> as</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">structure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SimpleGraph</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">extends</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Graph</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Sym2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">Adj</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">Adj_def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Adj</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsLink</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">symm</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Symmetric</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Adj</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">aesop_graph</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">loopless</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Std</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Irrefl</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Adj</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">aesop_graph</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I think it could replace the existing <code>SimpleGraph</code> definition pretty easily and inherit additional API from <code>Graph</code>. Is this something that would help in any way if I were to PR a change like this? Or is there some underlying design choice that explains why these structures should be entirely unrelated?</p>",
        "id": 604948398,
        "sender_full_name": "Gregory Wickham",
        "timestamp": 1781889534
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>What advantage do you see of using <code>extends</code> here vs writing a <code>def SimpleGraph.toGraph</code> function?</p>",
        "id": 604948774,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1781889628
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Nothing in particular. Would that be better just because it's simpler? (The only reason I went with extends is because it just makes more sense intuitively/semantically. I doubt it makes a practical difference though)</p>",
        "id": 604949370,
        "sender_full_name": "Gregory Wickham",
        "timestamp": 1781889800
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You are definitely welcome to experiment with it, it can't hurt to find the pros and cons of such a design and the community benefits from learning about it. However there are multiple ongoing efforts to generalize the graph theory part of mathlib, so I wouldn't recommend it from a practical standpoint. The answer to \"why is it this way\" is mostly just historical.  SimpleGraph has been around for years while <code>Graph</code> only landed in the last year or so.</p>",
        "id": 604949888,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1781889939
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>See <code>GraphLike</code> discussions:<br>\n<a class=\"message-link\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/263328-triage/topic/PR.20.214.234127.3A.20refactor.3A.20create.20HasAdj.20class.2C.20define.20Digraph.2E.2E.2E/near/597406040\">#triage &gt; PR !4#4127: refactor: create HasAdj class, define Digraph... @ 💬</a></p>",
        "id": 604950530,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1781890128
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If you want to help (thanks!), I think reviewing <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/36743\">#36743</a> and discussing pros &amp; cons in <a class=\"stream-topic\" data-stream-id=\"252551\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/252551-graph-theory/topic/HasAdj/with/599988235\">#graph theory &gt; HasAdj</a> is the way to go</p>",
